# Architecture

This document describes the architecture of the repository as a foundational governance model for ANS and LRN.

## High-level model

```text
                 ANS
                  │
        ┌─────────┼─────────┐
        │         │         │
   perception  reasoning  execution
        │         │         │
        └────── feedback ───┘
                  │
               learning
                  │
                 LRN
                  │
          validated knowledge
                  │
                 ISA
          integrity / audit
```

## Meaning of layers

### ANS
ANS is the operational nervous system. It is the runtime layer that senses, reasons, decides, executes, and learns.

### LRN
An LRN is validated operational knowledge that can be reused by an ANS during runtime.

### ISA
ISA is the integrity, audit, and governance framework. It provides the review and authority layer that constrains and audits operational behavior.

## Separation of concerns

These layers must remain distinguishable:

- ANS is operational.
- LRN is knowledge.
- ISA is governance and review.

They are complementary but not interchangeable.

## Why the separation matters

A validated LRN may inform operational decisions, but it does not automatically alter the governance structure or authority boundaries of the system.

An LRN must not become a backdoor to permanent authority or self-approval.

## Repository architecture

The repository is organized to support:
- LRN proposal and lifecycle tracking;
- validation records;
- audit and counter-audit records;
- evidence preservation;
- schema-based structural validation;
- peer review and governance.

